<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · song</p><h1 class="enc-title">Odes of Solomon</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">most scholars date them between <a href="/years/70">70</a> and 125 CE; estimates range from the 1st to the 3rd century CE</span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century">1st century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Odes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solomon">Solomon</a> are a collection of 42 odes, anonymous but long attributed to Solomon. Most scholars date them to between 70 and 125 CE, though estimates run from the 1st to the 3rd century and many settle on the 2nd. Earlier scholars thought they were first written in Greek or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew">Hebrew</a>; the consensus now is that the original language was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syriac">Syriac</a> or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aramaic">Aramaic</a> and that they came from the region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Discovery and study</h2><p>Portions of the Odes first became known through Pistis Sophia, a Gnostic text discovered in 1773, which led scholars to search for fuller copies. In 1909 James Rendel Harris found forgotten leaves of a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syriac">Syriac</a> manuscript on a shelf in his study; he could recall only that they came from the neighbourhood of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tigris">Tigris</a>. This manuscript, now Cod. Syr. 9 in the John Rylands Library, is the most complete text. It begins in Ode 3, the first two odes being lost, runs to the end of Ode 42 and continues into the Psalms of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solomon">Solomon</a>. It is a late copy, no earlier than the 15th century.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>James H. Charlesworth describes the key note of the hymns as joyous thanksgiving for the coming of the promised Messiah and for the present experience of eternal life and love. They call Christ the pre-existent Logos, both Son of Man and Son of God, name the Father, Son and Holy Spirit, and allude to the crucifixion and the descent into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hades">Hades</a>. They may hold the earliest non-biblical witness to the virgin birth, depending on their date. Their closeness to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gospel-of-john">Gospel of John</a> and to writings from Qumran has led Charlesworth to conclude that the writer was an Essene convert to the Johannine community. Thomas R. Schreiner reads their teaching on salvation as resting on grace and election.</p><p>Some scholars once proposed a Gnostic origin, pointing to the frequent use of the word knowledge and to unusual images such as the Father's breasts milked by the Holy Spirit. Henry Chadwick, Emerton and Charlesworth hold that the text has nothing to do with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gnosticism">Gnosticism</a>, and it is now often seen as proto-orthodox.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>The Odes were perhaps composed for worship. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syriac">Syriac</a> manuscripts every ode ends with a hallelujah, and plural commands suggest that a congregation is sometimes addressed. Commentators including Bernard and Aune find early baptismal imagery in them, with water an ever-present theme, though Charlesworth has led the criticism of that view. References to the Odes, perhaps even quotations, in the letters of Ignatius of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antioch">Antioch</a> are a strong argument for an early date. In 2010 the composer John Schreiner set them to modern music in The Odes Project.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Odes of Solomon” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
